1
00:00:08,964 --> 00:00:11,130
A harsh word is said in the morning.

2
00:00:11,210 --> 00:00:15,564
By evening, a grievance has a life
of its own. How did that happen?

3
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:18,988
I am John Tan, known as Thusness.

4
00:00:19,068 --> 00:00:22,946
These are my own notes, from my
own practice and contemplation.

5
00:00:23,756 --> 00:00:27,118
Four sections have looked at
dependent arising as a view.

6
00:00:27,198 --> 00:00:30,188
Whether things are
undeniably there, or formed.

7
00:00:30,268 --> 00:00:33,273
What a phenomenon is. Mind and matter.

8
00:00:33,353 --> 00:00:35,342
And the logic of dependency.

9
00:00:36,144 --> 00:00:40,127
All of that widened the word, from the
arising of suffering to everything that

10
00:00:40,207 --> 00:00:43,623
appears. But when the Buddha
speaks of dependent arising,

11
00:00:43,703 --> 00:00:46,870
he mostly means something
narrower, and more urgent.

12
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:49,972
How suffering comes
about. And how it ends.

13
00:00:50,788 --> 00:00:52,642
He gives it as twelve links.

14
00:00:52,722 --> 00:00:56,242
If I leave them out, I have left
out where the teaching starts.

15
00:00:56,322 --> 00:00:58,756
So this section turns to the twelve links,

16
00:00:58,836 --> 00:01:01,568
and stays with them as the
tradition teaches them.

17
00:01:01,648 --> 00:01:04,385
The next section reads
them with Nāgārjuna.

18
00:01:05,192 --> 00:01:07,752
Before the twelve, there is one sentence.

19
00:01:07,832 --> 00:01:10,243
The Buddha gives it again and again.

20
00:01:11,052 --> 00:01:12,811
When this is, that is.

21
00:01:12,891 --> 00:01:15,677
From the arising of this, that arises.

22
00:01:15,757 --> 00:01:17,946
When this is not, that is not.

23
00:01:18,026 --> 00:01:20,736
From the ceasing of this, that ceases.

24
00:01:21,544 --> 00:01:24,172
It is short, and easy to pass over.

25
00:01:24,252 --> 00:01:26,156
But look at what it does not say.

26
00:01:26,236 --> 00:01:28,343
It does not say that this makes that.

27
00:01:28,423 --> 00:01:31,550
It does not say a power goes
out from this into that.

28
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:33,425
It only says, when.

29
00:01:33,505 --> 00:01:37,356
Two things are named together,
in arising and in ceasing.

30
00:01:38,156 --> 00:01:40,037
Nothing is passed across.

31
00:01:40,117 --> 00:01:44,616
And because nothing is passed across, the
second half of the sentence is possible at

32
00:01:44,696 --> 00:01:49,063
all. If that had its own nature, it
would not cease when this ceases.

33
00:01:49,872 --> 00:01:53,112
Applied to suffering, the
sentence becomes a chain.

34
00:01:53,932 --> 00:01:58,490
Ignorance. Not knowing suffering,
its arising, its ending,

35
00:01:58,570 --> 00:01:59,880
and the way to its ending.

36
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:06,179
Formations. Intentional shaping,
by body, by speech, by mind.

37
00:02:06,972 --> 00:02:10,610
Consciousness. Knowing,
through the six senses.

38
00:02:11,416 --> 00:02:17,416
Name and form. Feeling, perception,
intention, attention, and the body.

39
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:21,966
The six senses. The fields
where meeting happens.

40
00:02:22,776 --> 00:02:27,202
Contact. Sense, object and
consciousness, meeting.

41
00:02:28,012 --> 00:02:31,569
Feeling. Pleasant, painful, or neither.

42
00:02:32,384 --> 00:02:36,987
Craving. Thirst, for more of the
pleasant, and an end to the painful.

43
00:02:37,788 --> 00:02:39,791
Clinging. Taking hold.

44
00:02:39,871 --> 00:02:43,912
Of pleasures, of views,
of rituals, of a self.

45
00:02:44,728 --> 00:02:47,850
Becoming. Taking on a mode of existence.

46
00:02:48,644 --> 00:02:49,844
Birth.

47
00:02:51,216 --> 00:02:55,393
And ageing and death, with
sorrow, grief and despair.

48
00:02:56,212 --> 00:02:59,234
In forward order, each
conditions the next.

49
00:02:59,314 --> 00:03:03,759
Such, the Buddha says, is the arising
of this whole mass of suffering.

50
00:03:03,839 --> 00:03:07,359
In reverse order, each ends with
the ending of the one before.

51
00:03:07,439 --> 00:03:10,244
Such is the ending of this
whole mass of suffering.

52
00:03:11,048 --> 00:03:14,854
As I read them, the forward order
describes how suffering runs while

53
00:03:14,934 --> 00:03:19,913
ignorance is present. The reverse order
describes how it ends when ignorance ends.

54
00:03:19,993 --> 00:03:23,500
A teaching that gave only the
first would describe a trap.

55
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:27,648
The twelve links are given so that
the trap can be seen, and left.

56
00:03:28,452 --> 00:03:31,823
It is natural to picture the
twelve as a line of dominoes,

57
00:03:31,903 --> 00:03:33,634
each knocking down the next.

58
00:03:33,715 --> 00:03:36,560
The discourses do not
keep to that picture.

59
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:42,144
In one long discourse, the chain stops
at consciousness and name and form,

60
00:03:42,224 --> 00:03:43,956
and turns back on itself.

61
00:03:44,036 --> 00:03:49,191
Consciousness depends on name and form,
and name and form on consciousness.

62
00:03:49,988 --> 00:03:54,654
The image given is two sheaves of reeds,
standing because they lean on each other.

63
00:03:54,734 --> 00:03:57,145
Pull one away, and the other falls.

64
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:02,939
A line of dominoes has a first domino,
and the first one has to stand on its own.

65
00:04:03,019 --> 00:04:05,452
Reeds leaning on each other have no first.

66
00:04:05,532 --> 00:04:07,998
No first point of the round is found.

67
00:04:08,812 --> 00:04:12,106
The tradition has read this
chain at more than one scale.

68
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:15,956
The classical reading
spreads it over three lives.

69
00:04:16,036 --> 00:04:18,714
Ignorance and formations in the past life.

70
00:04:18,794 --> 00:04:21,064
Consciousness through
becoming in this one.

71
00:04:21,144 --> 00:04:23,782
Birth, ageing and death in the next.

72
00:04:23,862 --> 00:04:25,888
This keeps rebirth at the centre.

73
00:04:25,968 --> 00:04:29,697
It is how most of the Buddhist
world has learned the twelve links.

74
00:04:30,492 --> 00:04:35,185
Another school allowed four ways of
taking the chain. In a single moment.

75
00:04:35,265 --> 00:04:36,789
Over a long span.

76
00:04:36,869 --> 00:04:38,636
As a connected series.

77
00:04:38,716 --> 00:04:41,440
And by stages, across lives.

78
00:04:42,256 --> 00:04:46,673
And some modern teachers, the Thai
monk Buddhadāsa best known among them,

79
00:04:46,753 --> 00:04:50,006
read the chain mainly as it
runs in present experience.

80
00:04:50,086 --> 00:04:53,489
Others have criticised this
as setting rebirth aside.

81
00:04:54,284 --> 00:04:56,450
I do not think these need to compete.

82
00:04:56,530 --> 00:04:59,417
They describe the same
pattern at two scales.

83
00:05:00,216 --> 00:05:02,908
Emptiness removes one
objection to rebirth,

84
00:05:02,988 --> 00:05:05,018
that it would need
something to carry over.

85
00:05:05,098 --> 00:05:06,879
Nothing ever has a carrier.

86
00:05:06,959 --> 00:05:09,556
But that does not prove
that rebirth happens.

87
00:05:09,636 --> 00:05:12,029
That is a matter for its own evidence.

88
00:05:12,109 --> 00:05:15,969
What I can say from practice is that
the same chain can be seen in a single

89
00:05:16,049 --> 00:05:17,249
afternoon.

90
00:05:17,836 --> 00:05:20,301
Someone says something
harsh to me at work.

91
00:05:21,104 --> 00:05:26,613
There is contact. The words, the ear,
the knowing of them. There is feeling.

92
00:05:26,693 --> 00:05:32,210
Unpleasant, sharp. Then craving.
For it to stop. For an apology.

93
00:05:32,290 --> 00:05:34,253
For the other to be shown wrong.

94
00:05:35,052 --> 00:05:38,305
Then clinging. To a view: I was wronged.

95
00:05:38,385 --> 00:05:41,172
And to the one who was
wronged. Then becoming.

96
00:05:41,252 --> 00:05:44,351
I take on a whole mode of
being, the injured party,

97
00:05:44,431 --> 00:05:46,679
with its history and its case.

98
00:05:47,488 --> 00:05:51,584
Then birth. By evening, the
grievance has a life of its own,

99
00:05:51,664 --> 00:05:54,423
rehearsed in the car, retold at dinner.

100
00:05:54,503 --> 00:05:58,567
Then ageing and death. It
hardens. It wears me out.

101
00:05:58,647 --> 00:06:03,341
In time it fades, leaving sorrow, and a
little more readiness for the next one.

102
00:06:04,148 --> 00:06:07,129
And behind it, ignorance and formations.

103
00:06:07,209 --> 00:06:11,803
The habit of taking the words, the speaker
and myself as things standing on their

104
00:06:11,883 --> 00:06:16,876
own. And the old shaping that had me ready
to react before the sentence was finished.

105
00:06:17,688 --> 00:06:20,873
Nothing in that afternoon
was made by one thing alone.

106
00:06:20,953 --> 00:06:23,310
And nothing in it was without conditions.

107
00:06:24,124 --> 00:06:27,984
When Ānanda told the Buddha that
dependent arising seemed plain to him,

108
00:06:28,064 --> 00:06:29,293
the Buddha stopped him.

109
00:06:29,373 --> 00:06:33,632
This dependent arising is deep,
he said, and it appears deep.

110
00:06:34,424 --> 00:06:37,202
The twelve links are not hard to memorise.

111
00:06:37,282 --> 00:06:40,449
What is hard is to see
them run, in a harsh word,

112
00:06:40,529 --> 00:06:44,638
or a pleasant memory, without someone
at the centre turning the wheel.

113
00:06:45,444 --> 00:06:49,458
This is Section 5, Part 1 of
the Dependent Arising series.

114
00:06:50,272 --> 00:06:52,031
When this is, that is.

115
00:06:52,111 --> 00:06:56,695
It describes how the knot is tied, and
it is also how the knot comes loose.

